An effect algebra is a partial algebra $(E;\oplus,0,1)$ with two distinct distinguished elements $0$ and $1$ and a partial binary operation $\oplus$ satisfying: (i) if $a\oplus b$ is defined then $b\oplus a$ is defined and $a\oplus b=b\oplus a$; (ii) if $a\oplus b$ and $(a\oplus b)\oplus c$ are defined then $b\oplus c$ and $a\oplus(b\oplus c)$ are defined and the two sums are equal; (iii) for every $a\in E$ there exists a unique $a'\in E$ with $a\oplus a'=1$; (iv) if $1\oplus a$ is defined then $a=0$. Define $a\le b$ iff there exists $c\in E$ with $a\oplus c=b$; then $\le$ is a partial order on $E$. The algebra $E$ is a lattice effect algebra if $(E,\le)$ is a lattice, and a complete lattice effect algebra if $(E,\le)$ is a complete lattice. For $a,b\in E$ write $a\leftrightarrow b$ iff $a\vee b=a\oplus(b\ominus(a\wedge b))$, where $b\ominus(a\wedge b)$ denotes the unique element $c$ with $(a\wedge b)\oplus c=b$. A block of $E$ is a maximal subset $B\subseteq E$ such that any two elements of $B$ satisfy $\leftrightarrow$. Set
$$S(E)=\{x\in E\mid x\wedge x'=0\},\qquad B(E)=\bigcap\{B\subseteq E\mid B\text{ is a block of }E\},\qquad C(E)=S(E)\cap B(E).$$
Describe explicitly the class of all lattice effect algebras, and the class of all complete lattice effect algebras, satisfying each of the following three conditions separately:
(a) $C(E)=S(E)$;
(b) $C(E)=B(E)$;
(c) $C(E)=S(E)=B(E)$.

